# Lab 10–11 — NIKE, Inc. pro-forma and sensitivity evidence

## Scope and source data

This model uses NIKE, Inc. as the company and USD millions except for per-share
values. FY2026 is the reported opening period. Reported FY2026 revenue was
$46,398 million, operating income calculated from the reported income statement
was $3,797 million, cash was $7,563 million, diluted weighted-average shares
were 1,481.0 million, and the effective tax rate was 20.3%. Source: [NIKE FY2026
Form 10-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/320187/000032018726000088/nke-20260531.htm),
Consolidated Statements of Income (p. 55), Balance Sheets (p. 57), and Cash Flows
(p. 58).

The FY2027E–FY2031E forecast is a transparent, simplified three-statement model.
It forecasts revenue, EBIT, NOPAT, working capital, PP&E, FCFF, a balancing cash
line, and a DCF enterprise value. The balance-sheet check is assets minus
liabilities minus equity. All displayed runs have a zero gap, and base was
restored and rerun at the end.

### Assumption table

The growth and margin paths below are labelled judgments for a gradual Nike
turnaround; they are not company guidance. The reported FY2026 starting point
supports using these two independent operating drivers: NIKE reported revenue of
$46.4 billion, 42.9% gross margin, and $17.7 billion of Direct revenue, which
was down 6% from FY2025. Its 10-K says the Direct decline reflected reduced
digital traffic, while gross margin was affected by product costs, logistics,
and channel mix.

