## Evidence-based result explanation

Over these specific ±1-percentage-point paths, operating margin is the larger
driver for FY2031E operating income, FCFF, and value per share. It has an EBIT
span of $1,096.8 million versus $594.1 million for revenue growth, an FCFF span
of $874.1 million versus $365.6 million, and a value-per-share span of $8.88
versus $3.18. The mechanism is direct: a one-point margin change applies to the
full projected revenue base and changes NOPAT and FCFF directly. Growth also
raises the revenue base, but it increases working capital and investment needs,
which offsets part of its FCFF effect.

This is a range-specific sensitivity result, not a forecast probability. Margin
is ranked higher here partly because both drivers use the same 100-basis-point
annual perturbation but have different economic paths through the statements. A
wider revenue-growth range could change the ranking. The base DCF value of
$47.74 per share is above the $36.62 comparison-date price recorded in the Lab 8
calculator, but that valuation conclusion should be treated as conditional on
the stated recovery and margin assumptions.

