## Learn-on-your-own responses

1. One-at-a-time sensitivity changes one independent assumption while holding the other independent assumptions at base, then recalculates every linked output. It isolates the model’s response to that stated change.
2. The ranking depends on both the model mechanism and the selected ranges. A wider range generally produces a wider output span, so a driver with a larger span is the larger driver only over the tested ranges.
3. A sensitivity table shows conditional model outcomes; it does not say how likely each input level is, whether the inputs move together, or whether the model captures every risk. It is therefore not a probability forecast.
